KG42.register("nodes-3fac5f0548244d7022db9c52",{"id":"COMP-f8b6dd7cb8a348ad","code":"PRC047","label":"Local dynamic pricing excluding network charges","definition":"Under the condition that, by the source assumption, local transactions do not bear the corresponding network charges, the price benchmark associated with the grid purchase price is adjusted downward to exclude that charge, forming a dynamic P2P electrical-energy unit price constrained by the grid-purchase-price upper bound.","layer":"L6","template_code":"BCT","concept_id":"pricing.local","concept_ids":["pricing.local"],"identity_dimensions":{"definition":"Under the condition that, by the source assumption, local transactions do not bear the corresponding network charges, the price benchmark associated with the grid purchase price is adjusted downward to exclude that charge, forming a dynamic P2P electrical-energy unit price constrained by the grid-purchase-price upper bound.","object":"Local P2P electrical-energy unit price; the applicable roles of the per-household purchase price and the community transaction price are retained separately.","inputs":"The time-of-use grid purchase price, the pricing benchmark specified by the source and its generation conditions, the network-charge adjustment rule, and the price boundary.","operation":"Determine the benchmark associated with the grid purchase price, then adjust it downward as specified by the source to exclude inapplicable network charges; the price boundary is retained as an independent requirement.","outputs":"A dynamic P2P unit price with a source-specified index, an applicable role, and price bounds.","boundary":"Limited to L6/BCT charge-exclusion pricing. The willingness-to-pay shadow price must not be equated with the grid purchase price, nor may the purchase price of 26 be extended to a price shared by the buying and selling sides; the arithmetic rule for 36% is not filled in, and no price truncation or feedback algorithm is added."},"exclusions":[["The benchmark is the willingness-to-pay shadow price obtained from independent optimization and cannot be directly replaced by the grid purchase price.","No storage and no interconnection constrain only the price-benchmark optimization; they do not constrain the actual community market that contains private batteries.","The specific arithmetic convention for the 36% downward adjustment is unspecified, and the multiplier is not filled in; the charge scope is network and grid-usage charges.","The output is made explicit only as a per-household, per-time P2P purchase price, and it strictly satisfies p_{p2p}\u003cp_G.","An exogenous dynamic grid-price sequence is used; distributed-generation investment cost is not counted, and price uncertainty is not represented.","The community configuration objective omits mutually offsetting internal P2P receipts and payments; a clearing-multiplier pricing cannot be additionally constructed on this basis."],["The community transaction price is indexed by time period and changes every half hour; the per-household willingness-to-pay optimization of 26 is not introduced.","p_{P2P}^{(t)}=(1−0.276)p_{DA_G}^{(t)} is a suggested value, and its condition of validity is that the transmission network is not used.","p_F^(t)≤p_P2P^(t)≤p_DA_G^(t) is retained independently; the interval and the suggested formula are not treated as equivalent, and no out-of-bound truncation is invented.","The original notation p_DA_G is retained, and other grid-purchase-price notations in the source are not unified unilaterally."]],"members":[{"id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R001::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","market_meaning":"Local trade prices are among the stated inputs to the community trading decisions optimised by the model; this does not assert that internal P2P prices enter the community objective as coefficients. Scope: Flexi User community trading decisions in the multi-period allocation model; qualitative description of decision inputs. The multi-period allocation model optimises community decisions in half-hourly intervals. Its objective minimises total grid-consumption costs, omitting internal P2P payments and receipts because they cancel at community level. Scope: Flexi User community allocation optimisation with decentralised private batteries. Renewable generation, grid consumption, battery discharge and P2P purchases together must be at least as large as household demand, battery charging and P2P sales together. Scope: Flexi User allocation feasibility for every house and model time step. Each house's aggregate P2P sales and purchases equal the respective sums of bilateral flows with other peers. Each bilateral purchase equals the corresponding sale multiplied by the P2P loss/conversion factor. Sold and purchased quantities remain within the community, and total loss-adjusted sales equal total purchases at every time step. Scope: Flexi User P2P allocations among community houses at each model time step. Condition: No grid feed-in is considered. Local allocations are subject to private-battery storage-level bounds, charging and discharging rate limits, and an efficiency-adjusted intertemporal storage balance. Scope: Flexi User allocation feasibility with privately owned batteries.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Flexi User P2P allocations among community houses at each model time step. | Flexi User allocation feasibility for every house and model time step. | Flexi User allocation feasibility with privately owned batteries. | Flexi User community allocation optimisation with decentralised private batteries. | Flexi User community trading decisions in the multi-period allocation model; qualitative description of decision inputs.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e06","v141-b-g01-e12","v141-b-g01-e13","v141-b-g01-e15","v141-b-g01-e16","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e19","v141-b-g01-e20","v141-b-g01-e21","v141-b-g02-e11"]},"constraint_refs":["26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"6ccdcd20acc1042ab8d05a86fbb39860768c442bfebd4499a922a592e1419e01","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R001","node_id":"pricing.local","function":"pricing.local","unit_id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R002::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"The dynamic local P2P price is applied as each house's purchase price for P2P exchanges. Scope: Flexi User P2P electricity purchases within the community; house-specific dynamic purchase prices. Peers may sell excess electricity from locally generated renewable energy to other peers within the community. Scope: Flexi User peer exchanges within the community. P2P sales and purchases remain within the community under the design's exclusion of grid feed-in. Scope: Flexi User P2P sales and purchases. Each house's total P2P sales and purchases aggregate bilateral flows with other peers. Bilateral purchases equal the corresponding reverse-direction sales adjusted by the distribution-loss and DG-conversion factor; total community purchases must likewise equal adjusted community sales. Scope: Modeled Flexi User P2P exchanges among community houses at each time step; bilateral flows concern distinct houses.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"activation","context_text":"Flexi User P2P electricity purchases within the community; house-specific dynamic purchase prices. | Flexi User P2P sales and purchases. | Flexi User peer exchanges within the community. | Modeled Flexi User P2P exchanges among community houses at each time step; bilateral flows concern distinct houses.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e03","v141-b-g01-e06","v141-b-g01-e15","v141-b-g01-e16","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g02-e16"]},"constraint_refs":["26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001"],"decision_anchor":"execution.energy_trade","source_rule_sha256":"f5f3489e0aeb1a9f8ab702d6f918da1e040043dfec31bf1372cea634f8ce8089","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R002","node_id":"pricing.local","function":"pricing.local","unit_id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","market_meaning":"The local P2P purchase price uses the house's willingness-to-pay shadow price from independently minimising each prosumer's electricity costs subject to the supply-demand balance, downscaled by 36% to exclude network and grid-usage fees. Scope: Flexi User local P2P purchase-price determination for each house and time step. Condition: The price-setting optimisation assumes no storage or interconnection; electricity traded locally is assumed not to incur grid-usage costs. The local P2P trade price is lower than the grid electricity price. Scope: Flexi User price assumptions for local P2P trade. Local P2P prices, as part of the model's non-grid prices, are determined from the exogenously given dynamic grid-price time series. Scope: Flexi User model assumptions governing local P2P price inputs. Local P2P prices exclude investment costs of distributed generation. Scope: Flexi User model assumptions applying to local P2P prices. The model does not represent uncertainty in local P2P prices. Scope: Flexi User model treatment of local P2P price uncertainty.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"Flexi User local P2P purchase-price determination for each house and time step. | Flexi User model assumptions applying to local P2P prices. | Flexi User model assumptions governing local P2P price inputs. | Flexi User model treatment of local P2P price uncertainty. | Flexi User price assumptions for local P2P trade.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e08","v141-b-g01-e23","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g02-e21"]},"constraint_refs":["26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C003","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C004","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C005"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","source_rule_sha256":"260e38816cf1a58d4acbd8d00576ccd66803100a0168b34441234de070e595d3","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005","node_id":"pricing.local","function":"pricing.local","unit_id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","market_meaning":"Local P2P trades use a dynamic price that varies half-hourly. Scope: Local P2P trades within the proposed community market. The design sets the local P2P price between the feed-in price and the grid-consumption price, inclusive: feed price ≤ P2P price ≤ grid price. Scope: Local P2P pricing within the proposed community market. A proposed reasonable local P2P price is the grid-consumption price minus transmission network charges of 27.6%; those charges should not apply under the stated local-trade condition. Scope: Proposed reasonable price for local community P2P trades. Condition: Electricity is traded locally without using the transmission network.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"Local P2P pricing within the proposed community market. | Local P2P trades within the proposed community market. | Proposed reasonable price for local community P2P trades.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e02","v141-b-g02-e07","v141-b-g02-e37"]},"constraint_refs":["32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C003"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","source_rule_sha256":"8829c33f9ccddfbdb0db6955615cc66fe68509a8d0c1214fd4d0c7a51b88540e","paper":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage","rule_ref":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005","node_id":"pricing.local","function":"pricing.local","unit_id":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true}],"paper_ids":["26-battery_flexibility","32-wholesale_prosumer_storage"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2 Model formulation","anchor_text":"four main operational supply-demand decisions: (1) consumers/prosu- am mers demand electricity from ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"To model the features of local trading, we focus on the interaction of four main operational supply-demand decisions: (1) consumers/prosumers demand electricity from the main grid, (2) prosumers use their own distributed generation, (3) P2P trading within the community, and (4) battery storage balancing. Hence, a community of prosumers and consumers faces trading decisions mainly based on RES surplus, battery flexibility, grid and trade prices. A multi-period linear programming model optimises these decisions in half-hourly time intervals (t) over a time horizon T. The objective function comprises electricity costs for the whole community and is subject to supply, battery and trade constraints.","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"To model the features of local trading, we focus on the interaction of four main operational supply-demand decisions: (1) consumers/prosumers demand electricity from the main grid, (2) prosumers use their own distributed generation, (3) P2P trading within the community, and (4) battery storage balancing. Hence, a community of prosumers and consumers faces trading decisions mainly based on RES surplus, battery flexibility, grid and trade prices. A multi-period linear programming model optimises these decisions in half-hourly time intervals (t) over a time horizon T. The objective function comprises electricity costs for the whole community and is subject to supply, battery and trade constraints."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e06","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"Table 1","anchor_text":"dem(t , h ) Demand of house h in time step t res (t , h ) Renewable energy production of house h in ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table 1","image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Table 1. Designated sets, parameters and variables of the mathematical model. Sets: t ∈ T — Hours t in time horizon T; h, p ∈ H — Houses h and peers p in community H. Scalars: \\psi^{P2P} — Distribution network losses and conversion of DG for P2P sale; \\underline{s}/\\bar{s} — Upper/lower bounds of storage level in battery; \\alpha/\\beta — Maximum charge/discharge rate of battery; \\eta^c/\\eta^d — Battery charging/discharging efficiency. Parameters: dem^{(t,h)} — Demand of house h in time step t; res^{(t,h)} — Renewable energy production of house h in time step t; p_G^{(t)} — Price of electricity from the grid in time step t; p_{p2p}^{(t,h)} — Price of electricity in the local market for house h in time step t; p_C^{(t)} — Price of charging the common battery in time step t; p_D^{(t,h)} — Price of discharging the common battery in time step t. Variables: C^{(t,h)} — Charge of battery of house h in time step t; D^{(t,h)} — Discharge of battery of house h in time step t; G^{(t,h)} — Grid consumption of house h in time step t; I^{(t,h)} — P2P electricity purchase of house h in time step t; I^{p}(t,h\\leftarrow p) — P2P electricity purchase of house h from peer p in time step t; S^{(t,h)} — Energy storage level in battery of house h in time step t; X^{(t,h)} — P2P electricity sale of house h in time step t; X^{p}(t,h\\rightarrow p) — P2P electricity sale of house h to peer p in time step t.","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"Table 1. Designated sets, parameters and variables of the mathematical model. Sets: t ∈ T — Hours t in time horizon T; h, p ∈ H — Houses h and peers p in community H. Scalars: \\psi^{P2P} — Distribution network losses and conversion of DG for P2P sale; \\underline{s}/\\bar{s} — Upper/lower bounds of storage level in battery; \\alpha/\\beta — Maximum charge/discharge rate of battery; \\eta^c/\\eta^d — Battery charging/discharging efficiency. Parameters: dem^{(t,h)} — Demand of house h in time step t; res^{(t,h)} — Renewable energy production of house h in time step t; p_G^{(t)} — Price of electricity from the grid in time step t; p_{p2p}^{(t,h)} — Price of electricity in the local market for house h in time step t; p_C^{(t)} — Price of charging the common battery in time step t; p_D^{(t,h)} — Price of discharging the common battery in time step t. Variables: C^{(t,h)} — Charge of battery of house h in time step t; D^{(t,h)} — Discharge of battery of house h in time step t; G^{(t,h)} — Grid consumption of house h in time step t; I^{(t,h)} — P2P electricity purchase of house h in time step t; I^{p}(t,h\\leftarrow p) — P2P electricity purchase of house h from peer p in time step t; S^{(t,h)} — Energy storage level in battery of house h in time step t; X^{(t,h)} — P2P electricity sale of house h in time step t; X^{p}(t,h\\rightarrow p) — P2P electricity sale of house h to peer p in time step t."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e19","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.2 Market specific constraints","anchor_text":"u- amount someone pays and the other one earns will cancel out, we leave ir these terms out of the o","equation_id":"(6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In the Flexi User market with decentralised storage, costs arise when a prosumer consumes from the grid or buys from a fellow peer. However, in the P2P trade, the selling peer earns money and thereby reduces the costs of electricity for the overall community. As the amount someone pays and the other one earns will cancel out, we leave these terms out of the optimisation. Thus, the objective function for this case minimises the sum of costs for grid consumption G^{(t,h)}, Eq. (6). \\min \\sum_h \\sum_t [p_G^{(t)} \\cdot G^{(t,h)}] (6).","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"In the Flexi User market with decentralised storage, costs arise when a prosumer consumes from the grid or buys from a fellow peer. However, in the P2P trade, the selling peer earns money and thereby reduces the costs of electricity for the overall community. As the amount someone pays and the other one earns will cancel out, we leave these terms out of the optimisation. Thus, the objective function for this case minimises the sum of costs for grid consumption G^{(t,h)}, Eq. (6). \\min \\sum_h \\sum_t [p_G^{(t)} \\cdot G^{(t,h)}] (6)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1 Market design for peer-to-peer trading","anchor_text":"he ability to sell excess electricity from renewable energy sources to peers. Each of the two setups","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Decentralised storage – Flexi User Market: This market design applies rules to the system setup with individually owned batteries. Prosumers and consumers within a community can trade locally produced energy at a dynamic local P2P price. Privately owned storage can be charged by DG from prosumers within the community.","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Decentralised storage – Flexi User Market: This market design applies rules to the system setup with individually owned batteries. Prosumers and consumers within a community can trade locally produced energy at a dynamic local P2P price. Privately owned storage can be charged by DG from prosumers within the community."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e16","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p07-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1.4 Electricity prices","anchor_text":"Fig. 3. Demand cover for exemplary houses on a scaled up to represent typical price levels customers","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"A reasonable price for P2P trade should reflect the willingness of each prosumer to pay for an extra unit of electricity under the assumption that there is no possibility for local trade or storage. The shadow price of each single prosumer can be seen as the willingness to pay of each prosumer. This approach is analogous to the proposition of Abbaspourtorbati et al. [45] who calculated clearing prices by the dual variables of the energy balance equations. To calculate the P2P prices, we independently minimise each prosumer’s costs for electricity subject to the supply-demand balance in Eq. (1) in the absence of storage and interconnection. The result of the separate optimisations provides a dynamic willingness to pay for electricity of each house in each time step. Since this P2P price should not consider the fees for network and grid usage (typically one third of the consumer bill, see [46]), we downscaled these prices by 36% as we assume that grid usage costs do not occur when electricity is traded locally in the community. Reflecting the willingness to pay for electricity, we implement these downscaled shadow prices as the house’s purchase price for P2P trade.","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"A reasonable price for P2P trade should reflect the willingness of each prosumer to pay for an extra unit of electricity under the assumption that there is no possibility for local trade or storage. The shadow price of each single prosumer can be seen as the willingness to pay of each prosumer. This approach is analogous to the proposition of Abbaspourtorbati et al. [45] who calculated clearing prices by the dual variables of the energy balance equations. To calculate the P2P prices, we independently minimise each prosumer’s costs for electricity subject to the supply-demand balance in Eq. (1) in the absence of storage and interconnection. The result of the separate optimisations provides a dynamic willingness to pay for electricity of each house in each time step. Since this P2P price should not consider the fees for network and grid usage (typically one third of the consumer bill, see [46]), we downscaled these prices by 36% as we assume that grid usage costs do not occur when electricity is traded locally in the community. Reflecting the willingness to pay for electricity, we implement these downscaled shadow prices as the house’s purchase price for P2P trade."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e06","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"Table 1","anchor_text":"dem(t , h ) Demand of house h in time step t res (t , h ) Renewable energy production of house h in ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table 1","image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Table 1. Designated sets, parameters and variables of the mathematical model. Sets: t ∈ T — Hours t in time horizon T; h, p ∈ H — Houses h and peers p in community H. Scalars: \\psi^{P2P} — Distribution network losses and conversion of DG for P2P sale; \\underline{s}/\\bar{s} — Upper/lower bounds of storage level in battery; \\alpha/\\beta — Maximum charge/discharge rate of battery; \\eta^c/\\eta^d — Battery charging/discharging efficiency. Parameters: dem^{(t,h)} — Demand of house h in time step t; res^{(t,h)} — Renewable energy production of house h in time step t; p_G^{(t)} — Price of electricity from the grid in time step t; p_{p2p}^{(t,h)} — Price of electricity in the local market for house h in time step t; p_C^{(t)} — Price of charging the common battery in time step t; p_D^{(t,h)} — Price of discharging the common battery in time step t. Variables: C^{(t,h)} — Charge of battery of house h in time step t; D^{(t,h)} — Discharge of battery of house h in time step t; G^{(t,h)} — Grid consumption of house h in time step t; I^{(t,h)} — P2P electricity purchase of house h in time step t; I^{p}(t,h\\leftarrow p) — P2P electricity purchase of house h from peer p in time step t; S^{(t,h)} — Energy storage level in battery of house h in time step t; X^{(t,h)} — P2P electricity sale of house h in time step t; X^{p}(t,h\\rightarrow p) — P2P electricity sale of house h to peer p in time step t.","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Table 1. Designated sets, parameters and variables of the mathematical model. Sets: t ∈ T — Hours t in time horizon T; h, p ∈ H — Houses h and peers p in community H. Scalars: \\psi^{P2P} — Distribution network losses and conversion of DG for P2P sale; \\underline{s}/\\bar{s} — Upper/lower bounds of storage level in battery; \\alpha/\\beta — Maximum charge/discharge rate of battery; \\eta^c/\\eta^d — Battery charging/discharging efficiency. Parameters: dem^{(t,h)} — Demand of house h in time step t; res^{(t,h)} — Renewable energy production of house h in time step t; p_G^{(t)} — Price of electricity from the grid in time step t; p_{p2p}^{(t,h)} — Price of electricity in the local market for house h in time step t; p_C^{(t)} — Price of charging the common battery in time step t; p_D^{(t,h)} — Price of discharging the common battery in time step t. Variables: C^{(t,h)} — Charge of battery of house h in time step t; D^{(t,h)} — Discharge of battery of house h in time step t; G^{(t,h)} — Grid consumption of house h in time step t; I^{(t,h)} — P2P electricity purchase of house h in time step t; I^{p}(t,h\\leftarrow p) — P2P electricity purchase of house h from peer p in time step t; S^{(t,h)} — Energy storage level in battery of house h in time step t; X^{(t,h)} — P2P electricity sale of house h in time step t; X^{p}(t,h\\rightarrow p) — P2P electricity sale of house h to peer p in time step t."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e16","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p07-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1.4 Electricity prices","anchor_text":"Fig. 3. Demand cover for exemplary houses on a scaled up to represent typical price levels customers","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"A reasonable price for P2P trade should reflect the willingness of each prosumer to pay for an extra unit of electricity under the assumption that there is no possibility for local trade or storage. The shadow price of each single prosumer can be seen as the willingness to pay of each prosumer. This approach is analogous to the proposition of Abbaspourtorbati et al. [45] who calculated clearing prices by the dual variables of the energy balance equations. To calculate the P2P prices, we independently minimise each prosumer’s costs for electricity subject to the supply-demand balance in Eq. (1) in the absence of storage and interconnection. The result of the separate optimisations provides a dynamic willingness to pay for electricity of each house in each time step. Since this P2P price should not consider the fees for network and grid usage (typically one third of the consumer bill, see [46]), we downscaled these prices by 36% as we assume that grid usage costs do not occur when electricity is traded locally in the community. Reflecting the willingness to pay for electricity, we implement these downscaled shadow prices as the house’s purchase price for P2P trade.","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"A reasonable price for P2P trade should reflect the willingness of each prosumer to pay for an extra unit of electricity under the assumption that there is no possibility for local trade or storage. The shadow price of each single prosumer can be seen as the willingness to pay of each prosumer. This approach is analogous to the proposition of Abbaspourtorbati et al. [45] who calculated clearing prices by the dual variables of the energy balance equations. To calculate the P2P prices, we independently minimise each prosumer’s costs for electricity subject to the supply-demand balance in Eq. (1) in the absence of storage and interconnection. The result of the separate optimisations provides a dynamic willingness to pay for electricity of each house in each time step. Since this P2P price should not consider the fees for network and grid usage (typically one third of the consumer bill, see [46]), we downscaled these prices by 36% as we assume that grid usage costs do not occur when electricity is traded locally in the community. Reflecting the willingness to pay for electricity, we implement these downscaled shadow prices as the house’s purchase price for P2P trade."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e08","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"Table 2","anchor_text":"P2P trade price (pp2p ) P2P trade price (pp2p ) Prices: pp2p \u003c pG Discharge price (pD) ca Charge com","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table 2","image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Flexi User — Prices: Grid price (p_G); P2P trade price (p_{p2p}); p_{p2p} \u003c p_G.","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"Flexi User — Prices: Grid price (p_G); P2P trade price (p_{p2p}); p_{p2p} \u003c p_G."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e21","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1.4 Electricity prices","anchor_text":"The optimisation and comparison of the proposed market designs al highly depend on the prices assume","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The optimisation and comparison of the proposed market designs highly depend on the prices assumed to the different sources of electricity consumption (see Table 2). We use an exogenously given dynamic price for the grid and determine all other prices based on this dynamic time series. The time series of the reference price data (RPD) for the UK electricity spot market is retrieved from the former APX Group. The RPD accounts for about one thirds of the electricity bill the end-users pay in the UK. Consequently, the RPD time series was scaled up to represent typical price levels customers have to pay at the residential level. The average of the adjusted RPD time series was set to the flat rate of 14.2 pence/kWh that the customers in the static pricing scheme of the Low Carbon London project had to pay.","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"The optimisation and comparison of the proposed market designs highly depend on the prices assumed to the different sources of electricity consumption (see Table 2). We use an exogenously given dynamic price for the grid and determine all other prices based on this dynamic time series. The time series of the reference price data (RPD) for the UK electricity spot market is retrieved from the former APX Group. The RPD accounts for about one thirds of the electricity bill the end-users pay in the UK. Consequently, the RPD time series was scaled up to represent typical price levels customers have to pay at the residential level. The average of the adjusted RPD time series was set to the flat rate of 14.2 pence/kWh that the customers in the static pricing scheme of the Low Carbon London project had to pay."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e23","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"Table 2","anchor_text":"P2P trade price (pp2p ) P2P trade price (pp2p ) Prices: pp2p \u003c pG Discharge price (pD) ca Charge com","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table 2","image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In addition to the rules on storage, trade and prices defined in the market design, we need to make some assumptions with respect to model complexity and computational effort. We assume unlimited supply from the grid at any time. From the physical perspective, we neglect battery degradation and characteristics of electricity distribution, i.e. load flow. Investment costs of DG are not accounted for in any prices. Although we are aware of uncertainty in generation from RES, we do not consider any uncertainty in production or prices.","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"In addition to the rules on storage, trade and prices defined in the market design, we need to make some assumptions with respect to model complexity and computational effort. We assume unlimited supply from the grid at any time. From the physical perspective, we neglect battery degradation and characteristics of electricity distribution, i.e. load flow. Investment costs of DG are not accounted for in any prices. Although we are aware of uncertainty in generation from RES, we do not consider any uncertainty in production or prices."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e02","location":{"location_id":"src-3c39fd6f72-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"market. In this paper, the market design allows P2P trade among ec- prosumers and consumers in a com","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In this paper, the market design allows P2P trade among prosumers and consumers in a community. Peers can trade locally produced and stored energy at a dynamic, half-hourly varying P2P price. Some prosumers and consumers have own batteries that can be charged with local generation and from the grid (see, e.g., [26]).","paper":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"In this paper, the market design allows P2P trade among prosumers and consumers in a community. Peers can trade locally produced and stored energy at a dynamic, half-hourly varying P2P price. Some prosumers and consumers have own batteries that can be charged with local generation and from the grid (see, e.g., [26])."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e07","location":{"location_id":"src-3c39fd6f72-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"Fig. 2. Flow diagram fo the local market are lower than the grid consumption price (i.e. • the price","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The source of electricity for infeed can only be DG or the residential battery, but to promote the local utilisation of distributed energy resources, local markets should consider a price structure with p_F^{(t)} ≤ p_{P2P}^{(t)} ≤ p_{DA\\_G}^{(t)}, i.e. feed price ≤ P2P price ≤ grid price.","paper":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"The source of electricity for infeed can only be DG or the residential battery, but to promote the local utilisation of distributed energy resources, local markets should consider a price structure with p_F^{(t)} ≤ p_{P2P}^{(t)} ≤ p_{DA\\_G}^{(t)}, i.e. feed price ≤ P2P price ≤ grid price."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e37","location":{"location_id":"src-3c39fd6f72-p09-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"p g g y p market receive for their small-scale installation ( \u003c 10 kW) be- tween 0.31 – 4.01 pence/k","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"(4) P2P prices: We propose that the chosen pricing schemes should always promote local trade first. For this reason, we set the P2P trade price in between the feed-in prices and the prices for electricity consumption from the grid, remember that p_{F}^{(t)} ≤ p_{P2P}^{(t)} ≤ p_{DA\\_G}^{(t)}. We find that a reasonable price would be the electricity grid consumption prices minus the transmission network charges of 27.6%. As electricity is only traded locally, the transmission network will not be used, thus the associated charges should not apply.","paper":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"(4) P2P prices: We propose that the chosen pricing schemes should always promote local trade first. For this reason, we set the P2P trade price in between the feed-in prices and the prices for electricity consumption from the grid, remember that p_{F}^{(t)} ≤ p_{P2P}^{(t)} ≤ p_{DA\\_G}^{(t)}. We find that a reasonable price would be the electricity grid consumption prices minus the transmission network charges of 27.6%. As electricity is only traded locally, the transmission network will not be used, thus the associated charges should not apply."}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["adjusted_willingness_to_pay_shadow_price","bounded_grid_benchmarked_local_price"],"projection_statuses":["clear"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"kind-L6-3798a5e7f6","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L6","ontological_kind":"adjusted_willingness_to_pay_shadow_price"},"semantic_definition":{"definition":"Under the condition that, by the source assumption, local transactions do not bear the corresponding network charges, the price benchmark associated with the grid purchase price is adjusted downward to exclude that charge, forming a dynamic P2P electrical-energy unit price constrained by the grid-purchase-price upper bound.","object":"Local P2P electrical-energy unit price; the applicable roles of the per-household purchase price and the community transaction price are retained separately.","inputs":"The time-of-use grid purchase price, the pricing benchmark specified by the source and its generation conditions, the network-charge adjustment rule, and the price boundary.","operation":"Determine the benchmark associated with the grid purchase price, then adjust it downward as specified by the source to exclude inapplicable network charges; the price boundary is retained as an independent requirement.","outputs":"A dynamic P2P unit price with a source-specified index, an applicable role, and price bounds.","boundary":"Limited to L6/BCT charge-exclusion pricing. The willingness-to-pay shadow price must not be equated with the grid purchase price, nor may the purchase price of 26 be extended to a price shared by the buying and selling sides; the arithmetic rule for 36% is not filled in, and no price truncation or feedback algorithm is added."},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","paper_id":"26-battery_flexibility","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"pricing.local","source_occurrences":[{"id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R001::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","market_meaning":"Local trade prices are among the stated inputs to the community trading decisions optimised by the model; this does not assert that internal P2P prices enter the community objective as coefficients. Scope: Flexi User community trading decisions in the multi-period allocation model; qualitative description of decision inputs. The multi-period allocation model optimises community decisions in half-hourly intervals. Its objective minimises total grid-consumption costs, omitting internal P2P payments and receipts because they cancel at community level. Scope: Flexi User community allocation optimisation with decentralised private batteries. Renewable generation, grid consumption, battery discharge and P2P purchases together must be at least as large as household demand, battery charging and P2P sales together. Scope: Flexi User allocation feasibility for every house and model time step. Each house's aggregate P2P sales and purchases equal the respective sums of bilateral flows with other peers. Each bilateral purchase equals the corresponding sale multiplied by the P2P loss/conversion factor. Sold and purchased quantities remain within the community, and total loss-adjusted sales equal total purchases at every time step. Scope: Flexi User P2P allocations among community houses at each model time step. Condition: No grid feed-in is considered. Local allocations are subject to private-battery storage-level bounds, charging and discharging rate limits, and an efficiency-adjusted intertemporal storage balance. Scope: Flexi User allocation feasibility with privately owned batteries.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Flexi User P2P allocations among community houses at each model time step. | Flexi User allocation feasibility for every house and model time step. | Flexi User allocation feasibility with privately owned batteries. | Flexi User community allocation optimisation with decentralised private batteries. | Flexi User community trading decisions in the multi-period allocation model; qualitative description of decision inputs.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e06","v141-b-g01-e12","v141-b-g01-e13","v141-b-g01-e15","v141-b-g01-e16","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e19","v141-b-g01-e20","v141-b-g01-e21","v141-b-g02-e11"]},"constraint_refs":["26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"6ccdcd20acc1042ab8d05a86fbb39860768c442bfebd4499a922a592e1419e01"},{"id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R002::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"The dynamic local P2P price is applied as each house's purchase price for P2P exchanges. Scope: Flexi User P2P electricity purchases within the community; house-specific dynamic purchase prices. Peers may sell excess electricity from locally generated renewable energy to other peers within the community. Scope: Flexi User peer exchanges within the community. P2P sales and purchases remain within the community under the design's exclusion of grid feed-in. Scope: Flexi User P2P sales and purchases. Each house's total P2P sales and purchases aggregate bilateral flows with other peers. Bilateral purchases equal the corresponding reverse-direction sales adjusted by the distribution-loss and DG-conversion factor; total community purchases must likewise equal adjusted community sales. Scope: Modeled Flexi User P2P exchanges among community houses at each time step; bilateral flows concern distinct houses.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"activation","context_text":"Flexi User P2P electricity purchases within the community; house-specific dynamic purchase prices. | Flexi User P2P sales and purchases. | Flexi User peer exchanges within the community. | Modeled Flexi User P2P exchanges among community houses at each time step; bilateral flows concern distinct houses.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e03","v141-b-g01-e06","v141-b-g01-e15","v141-b-g01-e16","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g02-e16"]},"constraint_refs":["26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001"],"decision_anchor":"execution.energy_trade","source_rule_sha256":"f5f3489e0aeb1a9f8ab702d6f918da1e040043dfec31bf1372cea634f8ce8089"},{"id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","market_meaning":"The local P2P purchase price uses the house's willingness-to-pay shadow price from independently minimising each prosumer's electricity costs subject to the supply-demand balance, downscaled by 36% to exclude network and grid-usage fees. Scope: Flexi User local P2P purchase-price determination for each house and time step. Condition: The price-setting optimisation assumes no storage or interconnection; electricity traded locally is assumed not to incur grid-usage costs. The local P2P trade price is lower than the grid electricity price. Scope: Flexi User price assumptions for local P2P trade. Local P2P prices, as part of the model's non-grid prices, are determined from the exogenously given dynamic grid-price time series. Scope: Flexi User model assumptions governing local P2P price inputs. Local P2P prices exclude investment costs of distributed generation. Scope: Flexi User model assumptions applying to local P2P prices. The model does not represent uncertainty in local P2P prices. Scope: Flexi User model treatment of local P2P price uncertainty.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"Flexi User local P2P purchase-price determination for each house and time step. | Flexi User model assumptions applying to local P2P prices. | Flexi User model assumptions governing local P2P price inputs. | Flexi User model treatment of local P2P price uncertainty. | Flexi User price assumptions for local P2P trade.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e08","v141-b-g01-e23","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g02-e21"]},"constraint_refs":["26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C003","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C004","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C005"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","source_rule_sha256":"260e38816cf1a58d4acbd8d00576ccd66803100a0168b34441234de070e595d3"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local trade prices are among the stated inputs to the community trading decisions optimised by the model; this does not assert that internal P2P prices enter the community objective as coefficients. Scope: Flexi User community trading decisions in the multi-period allocation model; qualitative description of decision inputs.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["theta_p_p2p"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e11","v141-b-g01-e06","v141-b-g01-e19"],"ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2 Model formulation","anchor_text":"four main operational supply-demand decisions: (1) consumers/prosu- am mers demand electricity from ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"To model the features of local trading, we focus on the interaction of four main operational supply-demand decisions: (1) consumers/prosumers demand electricity from the main grid, (2) prosumers use their own distributed generation, (3) P2P trading within the community, and (4) battery storage balancing. Hence, a community of prosumers and consumers faces trading decisions mainly based on RES surplus, battery flexibility, grid and trade prices. A multi-period linear programming model optimises these decisions in half-hourly time intervals (t) over a time horizon T. The objective function comprises electricity costs for the whole community and is subject to supply, battery and trade constraints."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e06","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"Table 1","anchor_text":"dem(t , h ) Demand of house h in time step t res (t , h ) Renewable energy production of house h in ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table 1","image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Table 1. Designated sets, parameters and variables of the mathematical model. Sets: t ∈ T — Hours t in time horizon T; h, p ∈ H — Houses h and peers p in community H. Scalars: \\psi^{P2P} — Distribution network losses and conversion of DG for P2P sale; \\underline{s}/\\bar{s} — Upper/lower bounds of storage level in battery; \\alpha/\\beta — Maximum charge/discharge rate of battery; \\eta^c/\\eta^d — Battery charging/discharging efficiency. Parameters: dem^{(t,h)} — Demand of house h in time step t; res^{(t,h)} — Renewable energy production of house h in time step t; p_G^{(t)} — Price of electricity from the grid in time step t; p_{p2p}^{(t,h)} — Price of electricity in the local market for house h in time step t; p_C^{(t)} — Price of charging the common battery in time step t; p_D^{(t,h)} — Price of discharging the common battery in time step t. Variables: C^{(t,h)} — Charge of battery of house h in time step t; D^{(t,h)} — Discharge of battery of house h in time step t; G^{(t,h)} — Grid consumption of house h in time step t; I^{(t,h)} — P2P electricity purchase of house h in time step t; I^{p}(t,h\\leftarrow p) — P2P electricity purchase of house h from peer p in time step t; S^{(t,h)} — Energy storage level in battery of house h in time step t; X^{(t,h)} — P2P electricity sale of house h in time step t; X^{p}(t,h\\rightarrow p) — P2P electricity sale of house h to peer p in time step t."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e19","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.2 Market specific constraints","anchor_text":"u- amount someone pays and the other one earns will cancel out, we leave ir these terms out of the o","equation_id":"(6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In the Flexi User market with decentralised storage, costs arise when a prosumer consumes from the grid or buys from a fellow peer. However, in the P2P trade, the selling peer earns money and thereby reduces the costs of electricity for the overall community. As the amount someone pays and the other one earns will cancel out, we leave these terms out of the optimisation. Thus, the objective function for this case minimises the sum of costs for grid consumption G^{(t,h)}, Eq. (6). \\min \\sum_h \\sum_t [p_G^{(t)} \\cdot G^{(t,h)}] (6)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The dynamic local P2P price is applied as each house's purchase price for P2P exchanges. Scope: Flexi User P2P electricity purchases within the community; house-specific dynamic purchase prices.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R002-P001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g01-e06"],"ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1 Market design for peer-to-peer trading","anchor_text":"he ability to sell excess electricity from renewable energy sources to peers. Each of the two setups","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Decentralised storage – Flexi User Market: This market design applies rules to the system setup with individually owned batteries. Prosumers and consumers within a community can trade locally produced energy at a dynamic local P2P price. Privately owned storage can be charged by DG from prosumers within the community."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e16","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p07-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1.4 Electricity prices","anchor_text":"Fig. 3. Demand cover for exemplary houses on a scaled up to represent typical price levels customers","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"A reasonable price for P2P trade should reflect the willingness of each prosumer to pay for an extra unit of electricity under the assumption that there is no possibility for local trade or storage. The shadow price of each single prosumer can be seen as the willingness to pay of each prosumer. This approach is analogous to the proposition of Abbaspourtorbati et al. [45] who calculated clearing prices by the dual variables of the energy balance equations. To calculate the P2P prices, we independently minimise each prosumer’s costs for electricity subject to the supply-demand balance in Eq. (1) in the absence of storage and interconnection. The result of the separate optimisations provides a dynamic willingness to pay for electricity of each house in each time step. Since this P2P price should not consider the fees for network and grid usage (typically one third of the consumer bill, see [46]), we downscaled these prices by 36% as we assume that grid usage costs do not occur when electricity is traded locally in the community. Reflecting the willingness to pay for electricity, we implement these downscaled shadow prices as the house’s purchase price for P2P trade."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e06","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"Table 1","anchor_text":"dem(t , h ) Demand of house h in time step t res (t , h ) Renewable energy production of house h in ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table 1","image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Table 1. Designated sets, parameters and variables of the mathematical model. Sets: t ∈ T — Hours t in time horizon T; h, p ∈ H — Houses h and peers p in community H. Scalars: \\psi^{P2P} — Distribution network losses and conversion of DG for P2P sale; \\underline{s}/\\bar{s} — Upper/lower bounds of storage level in battery; \\alpha/\\beta — Maximum charge/discharge rate of battery; \\eta^c/\\eta^d — Battery charging/discharging efficiency. Parameters: dem^{(t,h)} — Demand of house h in time step t; res^{(t,h)} — Renewable energy production of house h in time step t; p_G^{(t)} — Price of electricity from the grid in time step t; p_{p2p}^{(t,h)} — Price of electricity in the local market for house h in time step t; p_C^{(t)} — Price of charging the common battery in time step t; p_D^{(t,h)} — Price of discharging the common battery in time step t. Variables: C^{(t,h)} — Charge of battery of house h in time step t; D^{(t,h)} — Discharge of battery of house h in time step t; G^{(t,h)} — Grid consumption of house h in time step t; I^{(t,h)} — P2P electricity purchase of house h in time step t; I^{p}(t,h\\leftarrow p) — P2P electricity purchase of house h from peer p in time step t; S^{(t,h)} — Energy storage level in battery of house h in time step t; X^{(t,h)} — P2P electricity sale of house h in time step t; X^{p}(t,h\\rightarrow p) — P2P electricity sale of house h to peer p in time step t."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The local P2P purchase price uses the house's willingness-to-pay shadow price from independently minimising each prosumer's electricity costs subject to the supply-demand balance, downscaled by 36% to exclude network and grid-usage fees. Scope: Flexi User local P2P purchase-price determination for each house and time step. Condition: The price-setting optimisation assumes no storage or interconnection; electricity traded locally is assumed not to incur grid-usage costs.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["p_p2p","p_G","p2p_downscale_fraction"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e16"],"ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e16","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p07-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1.4 Electricity prices","anchor_text":"Fig. 3. Demand cover for exemplary houses on a scaled up to represent typical price levels customers","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"A reasonable price for P2P trade should reflect the willingness of each prosumer to pay for an extra unit of electricity under the assumption that there is no possibility for local trade or storage. The shadow price of each single prosumer can be seen as the willingness to pay of each prosumer. This approach is analogous to the proposition of Abbaspourtorbati et al. [45] who calculated clearing prices by the dual variables of the energy balance equations. To calculate the P2P prices, we independently minimise each prosumer’s costs for electricity subject to the supply-demand balance in Eq. (1) in the absence of storage and interconnection. The result of the separate optimisations provides a dynamic willingness to pay for electricity of each house in each time step. Since this P2P price should not consider the fees for network and grid usage (typically one third of the consumer bill, see [46]), we downscaled these prices by 36% as we assume that grid usage costs do not occur when electricity is traded locally in the community. Reflecting the willingness to pay for electricity, we implement these downscaled shadow prices as the house’s purchase price for P2P trade."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The local P2P trade price is lower than the grid electricity price. Scope: Flexi User price assumptions for local P2P trade.","expression":"p_{p2p} \u003c p_G","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["p_p2p","p_G"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e08"],"ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e08","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"Table 2","anchor_text":"P2P trade price (pp2p ) P2P trade price (pp2p ) Prices: pp2p \u003c pG Discharge price (pD) ca Charge com","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table 2","image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Flexi User — Prices: Grid price (p_G); P2P trade price (p_{p2p}); p_{p2p} \u003c p_G."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local P2P prices, as part of the model's non-grid prices, are determined from the exogenously given dynamic grid-price time series. Scope: Flexi User model assumptions governing local P2P price inputs.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e21"],"ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e21","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1.4 Electricity prices","anchor_text":"The optimisation and comparison of the proposed market designs al highly depend on the prices assume","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The optimisation and comparison of the proposed market designs highly depend on the prices assumed to the different sources of electricity consumption (see Table 2). We use an exogenously given dynamic price for the grid and determine all other prices based on this dynamic time series. The time series of the reference price data (RPD) for the UK electricity spot market is retrieved from the former APX Group. The RPD accounts for about one thirds of the electricity bill the end-users pay in the UK. Consequently, the RPD time series was scaled up to represent typical price levels customers have to pay at the residential level. The average of the adjusted RPD time series was set to the flat rate of 14.2 pence/kWh that the customers in the static pricing scheme of the Low Carbon London project had to pay."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local P2P prices exclude investment costs of distributed generation. Scope: Flexi User model assumptions applying to local P2P prices.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e23"],"ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e23","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"Table 2","anchor_text":"P2P trade price (pp2p ) P2P trade price (pp2p ) Prices: pp2p \u003c pG Discharge price (pD) ca Charge com","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table 2","image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In addition to the rules on storage, trade and prices defined in the market design, we need to make some assumptions with respect to model complexity and computational effort. We assume unlimited supply from the grid at any time. From the physical perspective, we neglect battery degradation and characteristics of electricity distribution, i.e. load flow. Investment costs of DG are not accounted for in any prices. Although we are aware of uncertainty in generation from RES, we do not consider any uncertainty in production or prices."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C005","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The model does not represent uncertainty in local P2P prices. Scope: Flexi User model treatment of local P2P price uncertainty.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e23"],"ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e23","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"Table 2","anchor_text":"P2P trade price (pp2p ) P2P trade price (pp2p ) Prices: pp2p \u003c pG Discharge price (pD) ca Charge com","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table 2","image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In addition to the rules on storage, trade and prices defined in the market design, we need to make some assumptions with respect to model complexity and computational effort. We assume unlimited supply from the grid at any time. From the physical perspective, we neglect battery degradation and characteristics of electricity distribution, i.e. load flow. Investment costs of DG are not accounted for in any prices. Although we are aware of uncertainty in generation from RES, we do not consider any uncertainty in production or prices."}]}],"variables":[],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"theta_p_p2p","symbol":"p_{p2p}^{(t,h)}","meaning":"Price of electricity in the local market for house h in time step t.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["t","h"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e06","v141-b-g01-e08"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R001::theta::theta_p_p2p"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-P001","symbol":"p_{p2p}^{(t,h)}","meaning":"Price of electricity in the local market for house h in time step t; the dynamic local P2P purchase price applied to house h's P2P exchanges.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["t","h"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e06","v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g02-e16"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R002::theta::v141-c-R002-P001"},{"parameter_id":"p_G","symbol":"p_G","meaning":"Price of electricity from the grid in time step t; an exogenously given dynamic grid-price time series in the Flexi User pricing assumptions.","value":{"kind":"text","number_value":null,"text_value":"exogenous dynamic grid-price time series"},"unit":"pence/kWh (UK calibration reference)","indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e08","v141-b-g02-e21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::theta::p_G"},{"parameter_id":"p_p2p","symbol":"p_{p2p}","meaning":"Price of electricity in the local P2P market for house h in time step t; implemented as the house's downscaled willingness-to-pay shadow price and constrained below the grid price.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"downscaled willingness-to-pay shadow price (exact arithmetic not stated)"},"unit":"same price unit as p_G (not separately stated in the rule evidence)","indices":["t","h"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e08","v141-b-g02-e16"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::theta::p_p2p"},{"parameter_id":"p2p_downscale_fraction","symbol":"36\\%","meaning":"Stated percentage downscaling applied to the willingness-to-pay shadow price to remove network and grid-usage fees before using it as the local P2P purchase price; the source does not state whether the operation is p*(1-0.36) or another downscaling convention.","value":{"kind":"numeric","number_value":0.36,"text_value":null},"unit":"fraction","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e16"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::theta::p2p_downscale_fraction"}]},{"unit_id":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","paper_id":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"pricing.local","source_occurrences":[{"id":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","market_meaning":"Local P2P trades use a dynamic price that varies half-hourly. Scope: Local P2P trades within the proposed community market. The design sets the local P2P price between the feed-in price and the grid-consumption price, inclusive: feed price ≤ P2P price ≤ grid price. Scope: Local P2P pricing within the proposed community market. A proposed reasonable local P2P price is the grid-consumption price minus transmission network charges of 27.6%; those charges should not apply under the stated local-trade condition. Scope: Proposed reasonable price for local community P2P trades. Condition: Electricity is traded locally without using the transmission network.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"Local P2P pricing within the proposed community market. | Local P2P trades within the proposed community market. | Proposed reasonable price for local community P2P trades.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e02","v141-b-g02-e07","v141-b-g02-e37"]},"constraint_refs":["32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C003"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","source_rule_sha256":"8829c33f9ccddfbdb0db6955615cc66fe68509a8d0c1214fd4d0c7a51b88540e"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local P2P trades use a dynamic price that varies half-hourly. Scope: Local P2P trades within the proposed community market.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e02"],"ref":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e02","location":{"location_id":"src-3c39fd6f72-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"market. In this paper, the market design allows P2P trade among ec- prosumers and consumers in a com","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In this paper, the market design allows P2P trade among prosumers and consumers in a community. Peers can trade locally produced and stored energy at a dynamic, half-hourly varying P2P price. Some prosumers and consumers have own batteries that can be charged with local generation and from the grid (see, e.g., [26])."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The design sets the local P2P price between the feed-in price and the grid-consumption price, inclusive: feed price ≤ P2P price ≤ grid price. Scope: Local P2P pricing within the proposed community market.","expression":"p_{F}^{(t)} \\le p_{P2P}^{(t)} \\le p_{DA\\_G}^{(t)}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R005-theta-pF","v141-c-R005-theta-pP2P","v141-c-R005-theta-pDAG"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e07","v141-b-g02-e37"],"ref":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e07","location":{"location_id":"src-3c39fd6f72-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"Fig. 2. Flow diagram fo the local market are lower than the grid consumption price (i.e. • the price","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The source of electricity for infeed can only be DG or the residential battery, but to promote the local utilisation of distributed energy resources, local markets should consider a price structure with p_F^{(t)} ≤ p_{P2P}^{(t)} ≤ p_{DA\\_G}^{(t)}, i.e. feed price ≤ P2P price ≤ grid price."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e37","location":{"location_id":"src-3c39fd6f72-p09-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"p g g y p market receive for their small-scale installation ( \u003c 10 kW) be- tween 0.31 – 4.01 pence/k","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"(4) P2P prices: We propose that the chosen pricing schemes should always promote local trade first. For this reason, we set the P2P trade price in between the feed-in prices and the prices for electricity consumption from the grid, remember that p_{F}^{(t)} ≤ p_{P2P}^{(t)} ≤ p_{DA\\_G}^{(t)}. We find that a reasonable price would be the electricity grid consumption prices minus the transmission network charges of 27.6%. As electricity is only traded locally, the transmission network will not be used, thus the associated charges should not apply."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"A proposed reasonable local P2P price is the grid-consumption price minus transmission network charges of 27.6%; those charges should not apply under the stated local-trade condition. Scope: Proposed reasonable price for local community P2P trades. Condition: Electricity is traded locally without using the transmission network.","expression":"p_{P2P}^{(t)} = (1 - 0.276)\\,p_{DA\\_G}^{(t)}","formalization_origin":"equivalent_translation","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R005-theta-pP2P","v141-c-R005-theta-pDAG","v141-c-R005-theta-tn-charge"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e37"],"ref":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e37","location":{"location_id":"src-3c39fd6f72-p09-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"p g g y p market receive for their small-scale installation ( \u003c 10 kW) be- tween 0.31 – 4.01 pence/k","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"(4) P2P prices: We propose that the chosen pricing schemes should always promote local trade first. For this reason, we set the P2P trade price in between the feed-in prices and the prices for electricity consumption from the grid, remember that p_{F}^{(t)} ≤ p_{P2P}^{(t)} ≤ p_{DA\\_G}^{(t)}. We find that a reasonable price would be the electricity grid consumption prices minus the transmission network charges of 27.6%. As electricity is only traded locally, the transmission network will not be used, thus the associated charges should not apply."}]}],"variables":[],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R005-theta-pF","symbol":"p_{F}^{(t)}","meaning":"Price of electricity fed into the grid (feed-in price) in time step t, used as the lower comparator for the local P2P price.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e21","v141-b-g02-e07","v141-b-g02-e36"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","declaration_ref":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::theta::v141-c-R005-theta-pF"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R005-theta-pP2P","symbol":"p_{P2P}^{(t)}","meaning":"Local P2P trade price in time step t; the source sets it between the feed-in price and the grid-consumption price and proposes a value of grid-consumption price minus 27.6% transmission network charges.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e21","v141-b-g02-e07","v141-b-g02-e37"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","declaration_ref":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::theta::v141-c-R005-theta-pP2P"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R005-theta-pDAG","symbol":"p_{DA\\_G}^{(t)}","meaning":"Grid electricity-consumption price in time step t as written in the supplied P2P price-ordering formula. The source's Table 1 labels a day-ahead grid price p_{DA}^{(t)} and Eq. (15) writes p_{G\\_DA}^{(t)}; the exact symbol reconciliation is not explicit in the supplied evidence.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e07","v141-b-g02-e37"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","declaration_ref":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::theta::v141-c-R005-theta-pDAG"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R005-theta-tn-charge","symbol":"0.276","meaning":"Transmission network charge component stated as 27.6% in the source's proposed local P2P price rule, used as the fractional reduction of the grid-consumption price.","value":{"kind":"numeric","number_value":0.276,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e37"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","declaration_ref":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::theta::v141-c-R005-theta-tn-charge"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","stage":"L6","subtype":"adjusted_willingness_to_pay_shadow_price","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Under the conditions of no storage and no interconnection, each prosumer's electricity-use cost of supply-demand balance is minimized separately, yielding a shadow price representing per-household, per-time willingness to pay; as stated in the original text, it is adjusted downward by 36% to exclude network and grid-usage charges, used as the P2P purchase price, and required to satisfy p_{p2p}\u003cp_G.","form_kind":"procedural","identity_fields":["Object: per-household, per-hour local P2P electricity purchase unit price","Core operation: independent benchmark optimization obtains the willingness-to-pay shadow price, followed by a charge-exclusion adjustment","Output: the dynamic price p_{p2p}^{(t,h)} used for house P2P purchase"],"variation_fields":["The price is based on an exogenous dynamic grid-price sequence","The specific arithmetic convention for the 36% downward adjustment is not explicitly stated, and the multiplier is not filled in","The benchmark optimization does not include storage or community interconnection","The price does not count distributed-generation investment cost","The model does not represent price uncertainty"],"constraint_refs":["26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C003","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C004","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C005"],"paper_id":"26-battery_flexibility","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"pricing.local","template_code":"BCT"},{"unit_id":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","stage":"L6","subtype":"bounded_grid_benchmarked_local_price","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"The local P2P unit price changes every half hour, and the price interval is p_F^(t)≤p_P2P^(t)≤p_DA_G^(t). For local transactions that do not use the transmission network, a pricing scheme deducting the transmission charge is proposed: p_P2P^(t)=(1−0.276)p_DA_G^(t); the interval requirement and the suggested value are retained separately.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: local P2P electrical-energy unit price","Core relation: the interval is bounded by the feed-in price and the grid electricity-purchase price, and a charge-deducted value of the electricity-purchase price is proposed","Output: time-varying p_P2P^(t)"],"variation_fields":["The price changes every half hour","The suggested deduction ratio is 27.6%, conditional on local transactions not using the transmission network","The charge-deduction formula is a suggested scheme; the original notation p_DA_G is retained, and other electricity-purchase-price symbols are not unified unilaterally"],"constraint_refs":["32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C003"],"paper_id":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"pricing.local","template_code":"BCT"}],"source_variants":[{"unit_id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","source_definition":"Under the conditions of no storage and no interconnection, each prosumer's electricity-use cost of supply-demand balance is minimized separately, yielding a shadow price representing per-household, per-time willingness to pay; as stated in the original text, it is adjusted downward by 36% to exclude network and grid-usage charges, used as the P2P purchase price, and required to satisfy p_{p2p}\u003cp_G.","retained_variants":["The benchmark is the willingness-to-pay shadow price obtained from independent optimization and cannot be directly replaced by the grid purchase price.","No storage and no interconnection constrain only the price-benchmark optimization; they do not constrain the actual community market that contains private batteries.","The specific arithmetic convention for the 36% downward adjustment is unspecified, and the multiplier is not filled in; the charge scope is network and grid-usage charges.","The output is made explicit only as a per-household, per-time P2P purchase price, and it strictly satisfies p_{p2p}\u003cp_G.","An exogenous dynamic grid-price sequence is used; distributed-generation investment cost is not counted, and price uncertainty is not represented.","The community configuration objective omits mutually offsetting internal P2P receipts and payments; a clearing-multiplier pricing cannot be additionally constructed on this basis."],"constraint_refs":["26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C003","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C004","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C005"]},{"unit_id":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","source_definition":"The local P2P unit price changes every half hour, and the price interval is p_F^(t)≤p_P2P^(t)≤p_DA_G^(t). For local transactions that do not use the transmission network, a pricing scheme deducting the transmission charge is proposed: p_P2P^(t)=(1−0.276)p_DA_G^(t); the interval requirement and the suggested value are retained separately.","retained_variants":["The community transaction price is indexed by time period and changes every half hour; the per-household willingness-to-pay optimization of 26 is not introduced.","p_{P2P}^{(t)}=(1−0.276)p_{DA_G}^{(t)} is a suggested value, and its condition of validity is that the transmission network is not used.","p_F^(t)≤p_P2P^(t)≤p_DA_G^(t) is retained independently; the interval and the suggested formula are not treated as equivalent, and no out-of-bound truncation is invented.","The original notation p_DA_G is retained, and other grid-purchase-price notations in the source are not unified unilaterally."],"constraint_refs":["32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C003"]}],"source_parent_definition":{"definition":"Under the condition that, by the source assumption, local transactions do not bear the corresponding network charges, the price benchmark associated with the grid purchase price is adjusted downward to exclude that charge, forming a dynamic P2P electrical-energy unit price constrained by the grid-purchase-price upper bound.","object":"Local P2P electrical-energy unit price; the applicable roles of the per-household purchase price and the community transaction price are retained separately.","inputs":"The time-of-use grid purchase price, the pricing benchmark specified by the source and its generation conditions, the network-charge adjustment rule, and the price boundary.","operation":"Determine the benchmark associated with the grid purchase price, then adjust it downward as specified by the source to exclude inapplicable network charges; the price boundary is retained as an independent requirement.","outputs":"A dynamic P2P unit price with a source-specified index, an applicable role, and price bounds.","boundary":"Limited to L6/BCT charge-exclusion pricing. The willingness-to-pay shadow price must not be equated with the grid purchase price, nor may the purchase price of 26 be extended to a price shared by the buying and selling sides; the arithmetic rule for 36% is not filled in, and no price truncation or feedback algorithm is added."},"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/292"});
